window.__finlexDocLoaded(10214,{"id":10214,"articles":[{"id":1000894,"no":"Overview","heading":"Scope & Core Objective (Public Summary)","text":"Jointly developed with the IASB (converged with IFRS 15), establishing the five-step revenue recognition model based on transfer of control of promised goods or services to customers.","seq":0},{"id":1000895,"no":"ASC 606-10-05 / 15","heading":"Core Principle, Scope Exclusions & Companion Topics","text":"Core principle (ASC 606-10-05-3 / 10-2): recognize revenue to depict the transfer of promised goods or services to customers in an amount that reflects the consideration to which the entity expects to be entitled in exchange for those goods or services.\n\n[Scope Exclusions (ASC 606-10-15-2)]\n• Lease contracts within ASC 842 Leases\n• Insurance contracts within ASC 944 Financial Services — Insurance\n• Financial instruments and other contractual rights/obligations within ASC 310, 320, 321, 323, 326, 460, 815, 825, and 860\n• Guarantees (other than product or service warranties) within ASC 460\n• Nonmonetary exchanges between entities in the same line of business to facilitate sales to customers (ASC 845)\n• Companion US GAAP Topics: incremental costs of obtaining/fulfilling customer contracts sit in ASC 340-40 (NOT inside ASC 606); sales of nonfinancial assets to non-customers sit in ASC 610-20.","seq":1},{"id":1000896,"no":"ASC 606-10-25-1~13","heading":"Step 1: Identifying the Contract, Collectibility & Contract Modifications","text":"[Five Cumulative Contract Criteria (ASC 606-10-25-1)]\n• (a) Parties have approved the contract (written, oral, or implied by customary business practices) and are committed to perform\n• (b) Each party's rights regarding goods or services to be transferred can be identified\n• (c) Payment terms for the goods or services can be identified\n• (d) Contract has commercial substance (risk, timing, or amount of future cash flows expected to change)\n• (e) Collectibility is PROBABLE — under US GAAP (ASC 450 convention), 'probable' means likely to occur (~75%–80%+ in practice), evaluated only for the consideration the entity expects to be entitled to (after implied price concessions).\n\n[Failed Collectibility (ASC 606-10-25-7)]\n• Cash received before Step 1 is met is recorded as a deposit liability; recognize revenue only when (i) no remaining obligations and substantially all consideration is received and nonrefundable, (ii) contract is terminated and consideration is nonrefundable, or (iii) control transferred, transfer ceased, and consideration is nonrefundable (ASC 606-10-25-7(c), unique US GAAP clarification).\n\n[Contract Combination & Modifications (ASC 606-10-25-9~13)]\n• Combine contracts entered into at or near the same time with the same customer (or related parties) when negotiated as a single commercial package, interdependent in price, or forming a single performance obligation.\n• Modifications: (1) Separate contract if adding distinct goods/services at SSP (25-12); (2) Prospective termination/new contract if remaining goods/services are distinct (25-13(a)); (3) Cumulative catch-up if remaining goods/services are NOT distinct (25-13(b)).","seq":2},{"id":1000897,"no":"ASC 606-10-25-14~22","heading":"Step 2: Identifying Performance Obligations & Shipping/Handling Election","text":"Identify each promise to transfer either (a) a distinct good or service (or bundle), or (b) a series of distinct goods or services that are substantially the same and have the same pattern of transfer (ASC 606-10-25-14~15).\n\n[Two-Step Distinctness Test (ASC 606-10-25-19~21)]\n• Capable of being distinct (25-19(a)): customer can benefit from the good/service on its own or with other readily available resources.\n• Distinct within the context of the contract (25-19(b) & 25-21): separately identifiable from other promises — indicators of NOT separately identifiable include significant integration service, significant modification/customization, or high interdependence/interrelation.\n\n[US GAAP-Specific Practical Expedients]\n• Immaterial Promises (ASC 606-10-25-16A): Entities need NOT assess whether promised goods/services are performance obligations if they are immaterial in the context of the contract.\n• Shipping & Handling Activities (ASC 606-10-25-18B): Entities may make an accounting policy election to treat shipping and handling performed AFTER the customer obtains control of the good as a FULFILLMENT ACTIVITY (accruing shipping costs when goods revenue is recognized) rather than a separate performance obligation.","seq":3},{"id":1000898,"no":"ASC 606-10-32-2~27","heading":"Step 3: Determining the Transaction Price","text":"Transaction price is the amount of consideration an entity expects to be entitled to in exchange for transferring promised goods or services (ASC 606-10-32-2).\n\n[Key Measurement Components & US GAAP Elections]\n• Sales & Similar Taxes Policy Election (ASC 606-10-32-2A): Unique US GAAP election permitting entities to exclude ALL taxes assessed by a governmental authority directly imposed on a revenue-producing transaction (sales, use, VAT, excise) from the transaction price without jurisdiction-by-jurisdiction principal/agent analysis.\n• Variable Consideration & Constraint (ASC 606-10-32-5~14): Estimate discounts, rebates, refunds, credits, price concessions, incentives, or penalties via expected value or most likely amount; include only to the extent it is PROBABLE (~75%+) that a significant reversal in cumulative revenue will not occur.\n• Significant Financing Component (ASC 606-10-32-15~20): Adjust for time value of money using a separate-financing discount rate; practical expedient ignores financing when transfer-to-payment gap <= 1 year (32-18).\n• Noncash Consideration (ASC 606-10-32-21~24): US GAAP explicitly requires measuring fair value of noncash consideration at CONTRACT INCEPTION (32-21); subsequent fair value changes due to the FORM of consideration (e.g., stock price swings) are excluded from the transaction price.\n• Consideration Payable to a Customer (ASC 606-10-32-25~27): Accounted for as a reduction of transaction price unless paid for a distinct good/service received from the customer at or below fair value.","seq":4},{"id":1000899,"no":"ASC 606-10-32-28~45","heading":"Step 4: Allocating the Transaction Price (SSP, Discounts & Variable Amounts)","text":"Allocate transaction price to each performance obligation in proportion to its relative Standalone Selling Price (SSP) determined at contract inception (ASC 606-10-32-28~31).\n\n[Estimating Standalone Selling Price (ASC 606-10-32-32~34)]\n• Best evidence: observable price when sold separately in similar circumstances to similar customers.\n• Suitable estimation methods when not directly observable: (1) Adjusted market assessment approach; (2) Expected cost plus a margin approach; (3) Residual approach — strictly limited to goods/services with highly variable or uncertain selling prices (32-34(c)).\n\n[Targeted Allocation Rules]\n• Allocating a Discount (ASC 606-10-32-36~38): Allocate proportionately across all obligations unless observable evidence shows the entire discount relates to only one or more specific obligations.\n• Allocating Variable Consideration (ASC 606-10-32-39~41): Allocate entirely to a specific performance obligation (or distinct good/service within a series) when terms relate specifically to efforts to satisfy that obligation and the allocation depicts expected entitlement.\n• Subsequent Changes in Transaction Price (ASC 606-10-32-42~45): Allocate on the same basis as at contract inception; amounts allocated to already-satisfied obligations are recognized as a cumulative catch-up adjustment in the period of change.","seq":5},{"id":1000900,"no":"ASC 606-10-25-23~37","heading":"Step 5: Satisfying Performance Obligations (Over Time vs. Point in Time)","text":"Recognize revenue when (or as) control of the promised asset transfers to the customer — control means the ability to direct the use of, and obtain substantially all remaining benefits from, the asset (ASC 606-10-25-23~26).\n\n[Over-Time Recognition — Meet Any ONE of Three Criteria (ASC 606-10-25-27)]\n• (a) Customer simultaneously receives and consumes the benefits as the entity performs (e.g., SaaS hosting, routine maintenance);\n• (b) Entity's performance creates or enhances an asset that the customer controls as it is created or enhanced (e.g., construction on customer's site);\n• (c) Asset created has NO alternative use to the entity AND the entity has an enforceable right to payment (cost + reasonable profit margin) for performance completed to date (25-27(c), 25-28~29).\n\n[Measuring Progress & Point-in-Time Indicators]\n• Progress Measurement (ASC 606-10-25-31~37): Apply a single output or input method consistently; 'right-to-invoice' practical expedient (55-18) permits recognizing revenue at invoiced amount when billing corresponds directly with value transferred; exclude uninstalled materials (zero-margin pass-through) and wasted costs from cost-to-cost measures.\n• Point-in-Time Indicators (ASC 606-10-25-30): Present right to payment, legal title, physical possession, significant risks and rewards of ownership, and customer acceptance.","seq":6},{"id":1000901,"no":"ASC 606-10-55-22~53","heading":"Implementation Guidance I: Principal vs. Agent, Returns, Warranties & Material Rights","text":"• Principal vs. Agent — Gross vs. Net (ASC 606-10-55-36~40): Determine for EACH specified good or service whether the entity controls it before transfer to the customer. Control indicators (55-39): (a) primary responsibility for fulfilling the promise; (b) inventory risk before/after transfer; (c) discretion in establishing price. Principals record gross revenue; agents record net fee/commission.\n• Sale with a Right of Return (ASC 606-10-55-22~29): Recognize revenue only for expected retained units, a refund liability for expected returns, and a return asset (carrying amount of inventory minus recovery costs, presented separately from inventory).\n• Warranties (ASC 606-10-55-30~35): Assurance-type warranties (promise that product complies with agreed specs) are accrued as a cost/liability under ASC 460/450; Service-type warranties (separately purchasable or providing additional service) are a separate performance obligation.\n• Customer Options for Additional Goods/Services (ASC 606-10-55-41~45): If an option grants a 'material right' (discount incremental to what other customers receive, e.g., loyalty points or discounted renewal), allocate transaction price to the material right and recognize when exercised or expired.\n• Breakage / Unexercised Rights (ASC 606-10-55-46~49): Recognize expected breakage (e.g., unused gift cards) proportionately as customers redeem rights if entity expects to be entitled to breakage; otherwise when likelihood of exercise becomes remote.\n• Nonrefundable Upfront Fees (ASC 606-10-55-50~53): Generally an advance payment for future goods/services unless tied to a distinct upfront transfer; administrative setup activities do NOT transfer a good/service.","seq":7},{"id":1000902,"no":"ASC 606-10-55-54~84","heading":"Implementation Guidance II: IP Licensing (Functional vs. Symbolic), Royalties & Repurchases","text":"• Licensing of Intellectual Property (ASC 606-10-55-54~64): Unique US GAAP framework dividing distinct IP licenses into TWO categories:\n  – Functional IP (55-59(a)): Significant standalone functionality (e.g., on-premise software, biological compounds, completed media/films) → POINT-IN-TIME recognition at the later of license transfer or start of license period.\n  – Symbolic IP (55-59(b)): Value depends substantially on ongoing entity activities supporting the brand (e.g., trademarks, franchise trade names, sports logos) → OVER-TIME recognition over the license period.\n• Sales- or Usage-Based Royalties Exception (ASC 606-10-55-65): When IP license is the sole or predominant item to which a royalty relates, recognize revenue ONLY at the later of (a) subsequent sale/usage occurring or (b) satisfaction of the performance obligation.\n• Repurchase Agreements (ASC 606-10-55-66~78): Forwards and call options are NOT sales — account as a lease (ASC 842) if repurchase price < original price, or a financing arrangement if >= original price. Put options depend on whether the customer has a significant economic incentive to exercise.\n• Consignment & Bill-and-Hold (ASC 606-10-55-79~84): Bill-and-hold revenue requires (1) substantive reason requested by customer, (2) product separately identified, (3) ready for physical transfer, and (4) seller cannot use or redirect the product.","seq":8},{"id":1000903,"no":"ASC 606-10-45 / 50","heading":"Presentation, Disclosures, ASU 2021-08 & SEC Focus Areas","text":"• Balance Sheet Presentation (ASC 606-10-45-1~5): Present a Contract Liability when payment is received (or unconditional right to payment arises) before performance; present a Contract Asset when performance precedes an unconditional right to payment; present a Receivable (ASC 310/326) when only the passage of time is required for payment.\n• Core Footnote Disclosures (ASC 606-10-50): (1) Disaggregation of revenue by economic factors (geography, product line, timing, contract type); (2) Opening/closing contract assets, liabilities, receivables, and revenue recognized from opening contract liabilities; (3) Remaining Performance Obligations (RPO / Backlog) with expected timing (practical expedients for contracts <= 1 year or right-to-invoice); (4) Significant judgments (timing, variable consideration, SSP).\n• ASU 2021-08 (Contract Assets/Liabilities in Business Combinations): Acquirers MUST recognize and measure acquired contract assets and deferred revenue under ASC 606 as if the acquirer had originated the contract (eliminating the former ASC 805 fair-value 'haircut' on deferred revenue).\n• Companion Contract Cost Rules: See ASC 340-40 for capitalization, amortization, and impairment (no reversal) of sales commissions and fulfillment costs.","seq":9},{"id":1000904,"no":"ASC 606 vs IFRS 15 / CAS 14","heading":"Three-Way Divergence Summary (US GAAP vs. IFRS 15 vs. China CAS 14)","text":"Although ASC 606 and IFRS 15 were jointly developed (and China CAS 14 closely converged), practitioners face recurring cross-border differences:\n• Step 1 Collectibility Threshold: ASC 606 'probable' (~75%–80%+) is higher than IFRS 15 / CAS 14 'probable' (>50%).\n• Shipping & Handling Election: ASC 606-10-25-18B allows treating post-control shipping as a fulfillment cost; IFRS 15 / CAS 14 have NO such election (often a separate performance obligation).\n• Sales Tax Exclusion Election: ASC 606-10-32-2A allows blanket exclusion of sales/VAT taxes; IFRS 15 requires assessing principal vs. agent per tax.\n• Noncash Consideration Measurement Date: ASC 606 fixes fair value at contract inception; IFRS 15 does not mandate inception date.\n• IP Licensing Model: ASC 606 uses Functional vs. Symbolic IP; IFRS 15 uses a 3-criteria right-to-access vs. right-to-use test (can yield different results for certain software/franchise licenses).\n• Contract Cost Impairment Reversal: Prohibited under ASC 340-40-35-6; required (up to carrying amount) under IFRS 15.104 / CAS 14.\n• Interim Disclosures & SEC Scrutiny: SEC registrants face intensive SEC comment letters on Principal vs. Agent (SaaS/marketplaces), RPO backlog, disaggregation consistency with MD&A/earnings calls, and ASU 2021-08 acquisition accounting.","seq":10},{"id":1000905,"no":"Official Access","heading":"Official FASB Codification & Reference Entry","text":"Official Portal (FASB Accounting Standards Codification®): https://asc.fasb.org/\nStatus: in_force (FASB ASC free Basic View available at https://asc.fasb.org/).","seq":11}]});
